# Production edge config for docs/DEPLOYMENT.md's routing rules (§5). Implements that document; doesn't
# restate its reasoning — read DEPLOYMENT.md first if a rule here looks surprising, it probably explains why.
#
# Status: not yet deployed anywhere (DEPLOYMENT.md's own status line: "planned, not yet built — no server
# exists to point DNS at"). Written and validated (`caddy validate`, see this file's own PR) against that
# plan so the actual VPS setup is "point DNS here and run this," not a rewrite.
#
# Service names below (server, website, storefront) match DEPLOYMENT.md §2's topology diagram — a production
# docker-compose (or equivalent) is expected to name its containers the same way; override via the {$...}
# environment placeholders if it doesn't.
{
	# DEPLOYMENT.md §3: Caddy's built-in ACME client needs the DNS-01 challenge for *.lytro.app's wildcard
	# cert, which needs a DNS-provider plugin (built in via xcaddy) and that provider's own directive here —
	# neither the VPS provider nor the DNS registrar is chosen yet (§10's own "open, deliberately deferred"
	# list). Fill in once both are: for example, for Cloudflare DNS,
	#   acme_dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
	# and rebuild this Caddy image with that plugin (xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare).
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# --- Shared pieces (DEPLOYMENT.md §5, §6), imported into every site block below instead of repeated ---

# /api/* -> reverse-proxy to the backend unchanged, with the edge secret attached (§6). Checked first, on
# every host: this is how the admin SPA (and anything else running client-side) reaches apps/server —
# same-origin, relative fetches to /api/v1/..., on whichever host the SPA happens to be running on. No path
# rewrite (API-14): the backend itself serves everything under /api/v1, the exact prefix the SPA already
# calls, so `handle` (not `handle_path`) passes the request through with nothing stripped.
(api_proxy) {
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy {$SERVER_UPSTREAM:server:3000} {
			header_up x-lytronix-edge-secret {$TRUSTED_EDGE_SECRET}
		}
	}
}

# apps/admin's static build: no process, Caddy serves dist/ directly (§8), falling back to index.html for
# any unmatched path so the client-side router takes over. Plain directives, not wrapped in its own `handle`,
# so this can be imported either as a handle block's body (lytro.app, *.lytro.app — only some paths go here)
# or directly into a site block (admin.lytro.app — the whole host does).
(admin_spa) {
	root * {$ADMIN_DIST_PATH:/srv/admin/dist}
	try_files {path} /index.html
	file_server
}

# lytro.app, www.lytro.app: marketing (apps/website) + tenant sign-up/sign-in/dashboard (apps/admin), split
# by path (§4, §5).
lytro.app, www.lytro.app {
	import api_proxy

	# Exactly the path list in DEPLOYMENT.md §5's "Correction, 2026-10-09" — kept in sync with that doc, not
	# re-derived from the route tree, since Caddy has no way to introspect apps/admin's TanStack Router config.
	@admin_paths path /sign-up* /sign-in* /verify-code* /forgot-password* /auth/oauth/*/callback /create-shop* /set-password* /unavailable* /dashboard*
	handle @admin_paths {
		import admin_spa
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy {$WEBSITE_UPSTREAM:website:3003}
	}
}

# admin.lytro.app: the operator console — same apps/admin build as every other host, showing its operator
# route tree instead of the tenant one purely because of which host it's running on
# (window.location.hostname in host.ts), not a different build. Everything but /api/* is the SPA.
admin.lytro.app {
	import api_proxy
	import admin_spa
}

# *.lytro.app (any other single-label host — a shop's own subdomain): apps/storefront (public) + apps/admin
# (staff dashboard), split by path. Covers every tenant automatically; no per-shop DNS entry or Caddy config
# change is ever needed for a plain *.lytro.app shop (custom domains are a separate, deferred mechanism —
# see the commented block at the end of this file).
*.lytro.app {
	import api_proxy

	@admin_paths path /sign-in* /dashboard*
	handle @admin_paths {
		import admin_spa
	}

	handle {
		# storefront reads Host itself to resolve which tenant to render (SFT-01) — Caddy passes Host through
		# unmodified by default, no explicit header handling needed here.
		reverse_proxy {$STOREFRONT_UPSTREAM:storefront:3001}
	}
}

# --- Custom domains (TEN-09, TEN-11, TEN-13, TEN-24) — deliberately not enabled yet ---
#
# DEPLOYMENT.md §7 describes this as fully automatic once live: a tenant's own domain (e.g. fashionhouse.com)
# resolves via HostClassifier's 'custom' branch (a database lookup, not a Caddy config change), with Caddy's
# On-Demand TLS issuing that domain's certificate the first time it's requested — gated by an "ask" callback
# to the backend before spending a Let's Encrypt rate-limit slot on it.
#
# Not wired in above because that backend callback doesn't exist yet (§7: "a small new endpoint... GET
# /internal/domains/:host/active or similar" — not yet built, same gap this file can't close on its own).
# Enabling on_demand_tls globally before that endpoint exists would let Caddy attempt — and rate-limit itself
# against Let's Encrypt trying — certs for arbitrary hostnames with no real check behind them. Once that
# endpoint ships, add:
#
# {
# 	on_demand_tls {
# 		ask http://{$SERVER_UPSTREAM:server:3000}/internal/domains/{host}/active
# 	}
# }
#
# and a catch-all site block (any hostname not matched above) that serves storefront the same way *.lytro.app
# above does, so a verified custom domain resolves exactly like a shop's own subdomain.
